WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.520
Hi! My name is Chris Gagné, and I'm an Agile coach.

00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:07.000
Today, I will talk about chickens and pigs.

00:00:08.060 --> 00:00:09.340
Like chickens and pigs?

00:00:10.080 --> 00:00:11.300
What's it got to do with Agile?

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:13.260
Well, here's the story:

00:00:13.900 --> 00:00:16.000
A Pig and a Chicken are 
walking down the road

00:00:16.520 --> 00:00:17.260
The Chicken says,

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:20.880
"Hey Pig, I was thinking 
we should open a restaurant."

00:00:21.320 --> 00:00:22.140
Pig replies,

00:00:22.740 --> 00:00:25.740
"Hmm. Maybe. What would we call it?"

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.520
And the Chicken responds,

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:28.980
"How about Ham and Eggs?"

00:00:29.860 --> 00:00:31.400
The Pig thinks for a moment and says,

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:35.800
"No thanks. I'd be committed, 
but you'd only be involved."

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.500
So it's a story about how committed
 someone is to the cause.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:43.060
The ham requires the pig sacrifice

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:45.780
while the chickens' eggs are easy to produce.

00:00:46.820 --> 00:00:49.420
Traditionally the development team were the pigs.

00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:54.760
While everyone else including: 
product owners, scrum masters, customers,

00:00:55.080 --> 00:00:56.520
and other stakeholders were chickens.

00:00:57.480 --> 00:01:00.140
The idea was that pigs got the priority

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:02.280
for talking during team meetings

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:08.120
Sometimes chickens were even expected 
to remain silent unless invited to speak

00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:12.420
because they weren't the ones 
who had their bacon on the line.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:15.420
Ken Schwaber, co-creator of Scrum,

00:01:15.900 --> 00:01:18.180
used to tell the story about chickens and pigs

00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:20.300
and it was even part of The Scrum Guide

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:24.760
However, Ken and its co-creator Jeff Sutherland

00:01:25.060 --> 00:01:28.100
removed it from The Scrum Guide in 2011

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:31.580
because it got a little too derogatory in practice.

00:01:32.360 --> 00:01:34.020
Developers might say something like

00:01:34.580 --> 00:01:39.680
The stakeholder can't talk to us during daily stand-up. They're just a chicken.

00:01:40.240 --> 00:01:45.240
It's true. We do want the development team to be able to focus on their meetings without getting derailed.

00:01:45.580 --> 00:01:50.180
But calling our leadership pea-brain birds 
isn't going to win us any favors.

00:01:51.160 --> 00:01:53.900
Since it hasn't been part of 
The Scrum Guide in quite a while,

00:01:54.440 --> 00:01:56.320
you may hear the term less and less often.

00:01:56.940 --> 00:01:58.320
That's perfectly okay.

00:01:58.820 --> 00:02:00.960
And it's still fine to use the term in your organization.

00:02:01.700 --> 00:02:04.440
Just don't use it as a chance to 
beat up on your stakeholders

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:06.380
If you really think about it...

00:02:06.760 --> 00:02:08.420
We've all got our bacon on the line.

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:10.480
Best of luck! Oink Oink

